    bottomLine:
      "The stain we hand to anyone nervous about ruining their cedar. It's nearly impossible to apply badly, it penetrates soft cedar the way an oil should, and the Natural Cedar tone flatters the wood instead of fighting it.",
    description:
      "Ready Seal Natural Cedar is the product we reach for first on cedar, and after a 12-month test on a southern-exposure cedar deck and a matching run of cedar privacy fence, it earned the top spot for one overriding reason: it is almost impossible to get wrong. Cedar is soft and porous, which means film-forming stains tend to sit on the surface and flake, while application mistakes — laps, overlaps, puddles — show up brutally on its open grain. Ready Seal sidesteps all of that. It's a penetrating oil that you flood on with a sprayer, roller, or brush and simply walk away from; there's no wet edge to maintain, no back-brushing, and no overlap marks even when we applied it in full sun at 85°F. On cedar specifically, the oil soaks deep into the thirsty fibers and the trans-oxide pigments knock down UV without masking the grain. The Natural Cedar tone reads slightly more amber than the raw wood at first, then settles over roughly 14 days into a warm reddish-brown that looks like cedar at its best. On the deck floor we saw light wear in traffic lanes by month ten; on the vertical fence, color was essentially unchanged at twelve months. Reapplication is the real payoff — a clean and a fresh wipe-on coat, no sanding, no stripping. It's the right pick for a first-time DIYer or anyone who wants cedar to look like cedar with the least possible drama.",
  },
  {
    id: "twp-1500-cedartone",
    name: "TWP 1500 Series Cedartone (1516)",
    category: "Oil-Based Semi-Transparent",
    badge: "Best Semi-Transparent",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6200,
    pros: [
      "Pro-grade semi-transparent oil that lets cedar grain read through clearly",
      "Cedartone (1516) tints toward cedar's natural warm reddish-brown",
      "EPA-registered formula resists mildew, mold, and algae on damp cedar",
      "Penetrates rather than films, so it weathers by fading not peeling",
      "Excellent UV package for a semi-transparent finish",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Two thin wet-on-wet coats required — overapplication leaves shiny spots",
      "Sold mostly online through specialty dealers, not big-box stores",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The deck-restoration pros' semi-transparent of choice for cedar. The Cedartone shade is dialed in for the species, and because it penetrates, recoating down the road is a wash-and-reapply job rather than a strip-and-sand ordeal.",
    description:
      "TWP 1500 Series is the stain you see on contractor trucks for a reason, and the 1516 Cedartone shade is purpose-built for the wood we're talking about. It's an oil-based semi-transparent that splits the difference perfectly for cedar: enough pigment to deliver real UV protection and that warm reddish cast cedar owners are chasing, but transparent enough that the distinctive grain and knots still show through. Over our 12-month test on cedar decking, TWP penetrated deep into the soft wood and the trans-oxide pigments held color noticeably better than the clear and lightly tinted products in the field — at twelve months the boards had faded gently and evenly rather than blotching or peeling. The EPA-registered formula matters on cedar because the wood holds moisture and the shaded, north-facing test boards are exactly where mildew likes to start; TWP showed no biological growth at the one-year mark where untreated control wood had begun to gray and spot. Application takes a little more care than Ready Seal — it goes on as two thin wet-on-wet coats, and if you flood it the way you would a true penetrating oil, you'll get shiny patches where the excess can't soak in. Wipe back any pooling within a few minutes. The tannin in cedar didn't bleed through or discolor the finish in our test. For the homeowner who wants a semi-transparent that shows the grain, fights mildew, and recoats easily, Cedartone is the benchmark.",
  },
  {
    id: "defy-extreme-cedar-tone",
    name: "DEFY Extreme Cedar Tone",
    category: "Water-Based Semi-Transparent",
    badge: "Best Water-Based",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 5400,
    pros: [
      "Zinc-nanoparticle UV blockers give exceptional fade resistance for a water-based stain",
      "Low-VOC, low-odor, and soap-and-water cleanup",
      "Cedar Tone shade preserves cedar's warm color without going opaque",
      "Resists mildew and stays color-stable on cedar longer than most water-based rivals",
      "Won't darken or yellow over time the way some oils can",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Cedar mill glaze must be removed first or penetration suffers",
      "Shorter wet-edge window than oils — work in manageable sections",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best water-based option for cedar by a clear margin. The zinc-oxide nano UV technology is the reason it holds cedar's color where ordinary acrylic stains wash out, and the low-odor, easy-cleanup formula makes it the eco-minded choice.",
    description:
      "DEFY Extreme is the water-based stain we trust on cedar, and the Cedar Tone shade is the one to buy. Water-based stains have historically struggled on cedar — they tend to sit higher on the surface than oils, and cheap acrylics fade fast under UV — but DEFY solves the UV half of that problem with zinc-oxide nanoparticles suspended in the resin, which act like a microscopic sunscreen over the wood. In our 12-month test on cedar decking, DEFY's color retention genuinely surprised us: at twelve months the Cedar Tone boards had held their warm reddish hue far better than any other water-based product we tried, fading only slightly and evenly. The trade-off with any water-based stain on cedar is prep. Fresh cedar carries a mill glaze — a smooth, slightly waxy surface left by the planer — and water-based formulas won't penetrate through it, so you must brighten and lightly sand new cedar first or the stain will bead and flake. Once prepped, DEFY soaked in cleanly and showed no tannin bleed or discoloration. The upsides are real for the right buyer: it's low-VOC and low-odor, it cleans up with soap and water, and unlike some oils it won't darken or amber as it ages, so the cedar tone you apply is the tone you keep. Recoating is straightforward — clean, brighten, and apply a maintenance coat. For homeowners who want easy cleanup and the lowest odor without giving up color life, DEFY Extreme Cedar Tone is the standout.",
  },
  {
    id: "cabot-australian-timber-oil",
    name: "Cabot Australian Timber Oil",
    category: "Oil-Based Penetrating (Semi-Transparent)",
    badge: "Best Oil-Based",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 9100,
    pros: [
      "Rich blend of linseed and tung-based oils penetrates deep into soft cedar",
      "Adds a warm, slightly amber glow that flatters cedar's natural red tones",
      "Translucent iron-oxide pigments deliver strong UV protection",
      "Brings out depth and figure in the grain better than any product we tested",
      "Single-coat application keeps the look natural, not heavy",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Warms the color noticeably — verify the tone on a test board first",
      "Slow oil cure; keep traffic and water off for 24-48 hours",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If your goal is to make cedar look as rich and warm as it possibly can, Australian Timber Oil is the stain that gets you there. The oil blend feeds the wood and the iron-oxide pigments make the grain glow.",
    description:
      "Cabot Australian Timber Oil was formulated for dense exotic hardwoods, but it has quietly become one of the best things you can put on cedar, and our 12-month test confirmed why. It's a penetrating oil built around a blend of linseed and tung-type oils that soak deep into cedar's open, thirsty fibers and effectively feed the wood, restoring the supple, slightly oily character that weathered cedar loses. The translucent iron-oxide pigments do double duty: they screen UV to slow graying, and they add a warm, faintly amber depth that makes cedar's natural reddish undertones glow. Of everything in this guide, Australian Timber Oil produced the most dramatic instant transformation on day one — the grain figure and color depth it pulls out of tired cedar is genuinely impressive. Over the year, it weathered the way a good penetrating oil should, fading gradually and evenly without a single flake or peel; the vertical fence boards held color better than the horizontal deck, as expected. The honest caution is that warming effect: on already-red cedar it can push the tone toward a deeper russet, so we strongly recommend wiping a test board and living with it for a day before committing. We saw no tannin bleed or blotching. Because it penetrates rather than films, a single coat is usually correct and recoating is a clean-and-reapply affair. For the buyer chasing maximum richness and warmth on cedar, this is the oil to beat.",
  },
  {
    id: "olympic-elite-semi-transparent",
    name: "Olympic Elite Semi-Transparent Stain & Sealant",
    category: "Water-Based Semi-Transparent",
    badge: "Best Big-Box Pick",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 7600,
    pros: [
      "Widely available at major home centers in cedar-friendly tones",
      "Advanced acrylic resin resists cracking, peeling, and color fade",
      "Tones such as Cedar Naturaltone keep cedar's warm character intact",
      "Mildew-resistant coating and water beading hold up on damp cedar",
      "One-coat application with a stain-and-sealer in a single product",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Surface-leaning film needs thorough cedar prep to bond well",
      "Color reads a touch flatter than penetrating oils on cedar grain",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The convenience pick. You can buy it tinted to a cedar tone at the same store you buy your brushes, and the advanced acrylic resin gives respectable color and water protection without mail-ordering a specialty product.",
    description:
      "Olympic Elite is the stain for the homeowner who wants a solid cedar finish without ordering anything online, and in our 12-month test it justified its big-box shelf space. It's a water-based semi-transparent stain-and-sealer built on an advanced acrylic resin, and in cedar-appropriate shades like Cedar Naturaltone it preserves the warm, reddish character of the wood while still letting grain and texture show through. As a one-coat stain-and-sealer it simplifies the job — you're applying color and water repellency in a single pass — and on our prepped cedar deck it laid down evenly with good water beading that survived the full year. Because Olympic Elite leans more toward a surface film than a deep-penetrating oil, prep is what separates a good result from a disappointing one on cedar: the mill glaze on new boards and any graying on old ones must be cleaned and brightened (and new cedar lightly sanded) so the acrylic can grip. Where we cut corners on a prep test panel, the stain wore through faster in the traffic lane. Properly prepped, color retention at twelve months was good for a water-based product, fading modestly and evenly with no peeling and no tannin discoloration. The trade-off versus the penetrating oils is purely aesthetic — the grain reads slightly flatter, a bit more coated-looking and less deeply fed. For most homeowners that difference is minor and well worth the convenience of grabbing it, pre-tinted, on the same trip as the rest of the project.",
  },
  {
    id: "thompsons-waterseal-timber-oil",
    name: "Thompson's WaterSeal Penetrating Timber Oil",
    category: "Penetrating Oil (Transparent/Tinted)",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 4800,
    pros: [
      "Lowest cost per square foot of anything we tested on cedar",
      "Penetrating oil soaks into soft cedar and won't peel",
      "Available in a warm cedar-leaning tint that preserves the natural color",
      "Adds genuine water repellency and some UV protection in one coat",
      "Simple, forgiving one-coat application",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Lighter pigment load means shorter color life — expect annual recoats",
      "Less mildew resistance than premium oils in damp, shaded spots",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "When budget is the deciding factor, this penetrating timber oil delivers real water repellency and a warm cedar tone for the least money. You'll recoat more often, but each coat is cheap and easy.",
    description:
      "Thompson's WaterSeal Penetrating Timber Oil is the value play, and on cedar it's a more sensible choice than the brand's better-known clear waterproofers. This is an actual penetrating oil with pigment, not a bargain clear sealer, so it soaks into cedar's soft fibers, adds genuine water repellency, and — in the warmer cedar-leaning tints — preserves the wood's natural reddish color rather than leaving it to gray. In our 12-month test it did exactly what a budget penetrating oil should: it went on easily in a single forgiving coat, beaded water well through the first two seasons, and never peeled or flaked because there's no film to fail. The honest limitation is color life. The pigment load is lighter than premium products like TWP or Australian Timber Oil, so UV protection is more modest and the warm tone faded faster — by the twelve-month mark the deck boards had grayed noticeably more than the higher-pigment stains, and the shaded boards showed the early edge of mildew that the EPA-registered oils resisted. The practical answer is to treat this as an annual-maintenance product: because each coat is inexpensive and trivially easy to reapply (clean and wipe on), a once-a-year refresh keeps cedar looking warm for a fraction of the cost of a premium stain applied every three years. For rental properties, large fences, or anyone watching the budget closely, it's honest value.",
  },
  {
    id: "armstrong-clark-cedar",
    name: "Armstrong-Clark Cedar (Semi-Transparent)",
    category: "Oil-Based Semi-Transparent",
    badge: "Best for Vertical/Siding",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1900,
    pros: [
      "Unique drying-and-non-drying oil blend lets it penetrate before it sets",
      "Exceptional on vertical cedar — siding, fences, and railings",
      "Cedar shade holds a warm, natural reddish tone with strong UV resistance",
      "Long open time means no laps or overlap marks on big runs of siding",
      "Penetrates deeply and recoats without stripping",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Niche, online-only availability through specialty retailers",
      "Premium price relative to big-box stains",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Cedar isn't just decking — it's siding, fences, and shakes, and Armstrong-Clark is the stain built for those vertical surfaces. Its split oil chemistry penetrates before it dries, so it conditions the wood and resists UV beautifully on the sides of a house.",
    description:
      "Armstrong-Clark is a specialty stain with a clever trick, and on vertical cedar it's the best thing we tested. Its formula combines drying oils and non-drying oils: the non-drying oils keep penetrating into the wood while the drying oils cure on top, so the stain conditions cedar deeply rather than skimming the surface — a real advantage on the soft, thirsty fibers of cedar siding and fencing. We ran the Cedar shade on a section of cedar privacy fence and cedar lap siding for twelve months, and it excelled exactly where vertical cedar lives: it held a warm, natural reddish tone with strong UV resistance, and because the oil has a long open time, we got through long runs of siding with zero lap marks or overlap streaks. That matters enormously on a wall, where the eye catches every inconsistency in raking light. On vertical surfaces — which shed water and see less abrasion than a deck floor — the color held nearly as fresh at twelve months as the day we applied it, outperforming everything else in the field for siding and fence use. It works on cedar decking too, but its sweet spot is clearly vertical. There was no tannin bleed and no mildew on the shaded boards. The drawbacks are practical rather than performance-based: it's online-only through deck-stain specialists and it costs more than a big-box can. For cedar siding, shakes, fences, and railings, it's worth seeking out.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Lists", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Deck Stain for Cedar", item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-deck-stain-for-cedar/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best stain for a cedar deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most cedar decks, a penetrating semi-transparent stain in a cedar tone is the best choice — it preserves the wood's natural reddish color while delivering real UV protection. Ready Seal Natural Cedar is our top overall pick for its foolproof application and deep penetration into soft cedar. TWP 1500 Cedartone is the best traditional semi-transparent, and DEFY Extreme Cedar Tone is the best water-based option. Transparent and clear sealers look great briefly but gray quickly because they lack the pigment cedar needs for UV defense.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I use a transparent or semi-transparent stain on cedar?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Semi-transparent is the sweet spot for cedar. Transparent and clear finishes show off the grain beautifully but carry too little pigment to block UV, so cedar grays within a season or two. Solid stains last longer but hide the very grain and warm color that make cedar worth having. Semi-transparent stains keep the grain and knots visible while adding enough trans-oxide pigment to slow graying and preserve cedar's reddish-brown tone for years.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is oil-based or water-based stain better for cedar?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Oil-based penetrating stains traditionally suit cedar best because they soak deep into the soft, porous fibers, condition the wood, and weather by fading rather than peeling — products like TWP, Cabot Australian Timber Oil, and Armstrong-Clark excel here. Modern water-based stains have closed much of the gap: DEFY Extreme uses zinc-oxide nano UV blockers to hold cedar's color while offering low odor and soap-and-water cleanup. Choose oil for maximum penetration and richness, water-based for low VOCs and easy cleanup.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I keep my cedar deck from turning gray?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Graying is UV damage to the wood's surface fibers. To prevent it, use a pigmented stain — clear sealers won't stop it. Pick a semi-transparent stain with trans-oxide or iron-oxide pigments in a cedar tone, keep the deck clean so mildew and dirt don't trap moisture, and reapply on schedule (every 1-3 years depending on the product and exposure). If your cedar has already grayed, a wood brightener or oxalic-acid cleaner restores much of the original color before you stain.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do I need to wait before staining a new cedar deck?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes. New cedar usually needs a few weeks to dry to under about 15 percent moisture content so the stain can penetrate, and it carries a smooth mill glaze from the planer that repels stain. Clean and brighten the wood, then lightly sand to break the mill glaze and open the grain before staining. Test absorption by sprinkling water on the board — if it beads instead of soaking in, the wood isn't ready and the stain won't penetrate either.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-06-22",
      dateModified: "2026-06-22",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      aggregateRating: { "@type": "AggregateRating", ratingValue: product.rating, reviewCount: product.reviewCount },
    })),
