---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | The Honest Reviewers`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-06-22",
    modifiedTime: "2026-06-22",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: ["cedar deck stain", "deck stain", "cedar", "wood stain", "home improvement"],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our #1 Pick", link: "#ready-seal-natural-cedar" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">12-Month Real-World Test</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best Deck Stains for <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Cedar Decks</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Cedar is soft, tannin-rich, and races to gray the moment the sun hits it. We tested transparent, semi-transparent, oil, and water-based stains on real cedar decking and fencing for a full year. Here's what preserved that natural reddish glow — and what let it fade.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#ready-seal-natural-cedar" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">See Our #1 Pick</a>
          <a href="#how-to-stain" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">Application Guide</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">June 22, 2026</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto"><SocialProof users="8.7k" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4">In This Guide</h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-cedar" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Cedar Needs a Different Approach</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Our 7 Top Cedar Stains Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#how-to-stain" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. How to Stain a Cedar Deck</a>
          <a href="#prep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Surface Prep for Cedar</a>
          <a href="#oil-vs-water" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Oil vs Water-Based on Cedar</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
          Cedar is one of the most beautiful — and most fragile — decking woods you can own. It's soft, full of natural tannins and oils, and it grays faster than almost any other species when the sun gets to it. The right stain preserves that warm reddish glow for years. The wrong one peels, blotches, or lets the wood silver out by August.
        </p>

        <div id="why-cedar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Cedar Needs a Different Approach
          </h2>
          <p>
            Cedar isn't pressure-treated pine and it isn't a dense tropical hardwood — it's its own thing, and the stain that works on a treated-lumber deck can actively ruin a cedar one. Three properties of the wood drive every recommendation in this guide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It's Soft and Porous</h3>
          <p>
            Western red cedar is a lightweight, low-density softwood with open, thirsty grain. That porosity is an advantage for penetrating oils, which soak deep and lock in — but a liability for film-forming finishes, which sit on top of the soft surface and crack or peel as the wood moves. On cedar, <strong>penetration beats film</strong> almost every time, which is why the best products here are penetrating oils and thin semi-transparents rather than heavy coatings.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It's Full of Tannins and Natural Oils</h3>
          <p>
            Cedar's natural tannins and extractives are what give it rot resistance and that distinctive smell — but they can bleed, especially under water-based film finishes and especially around knots, leaving dark streaks. Penetrating oil stains live in harmony with cedar's own oils; they don't trap the tannins under a film. In our testing, the penetrating oils showed <strong>no tannin bleed at all</strong>, while we've seen acrylic films on cedar discolor around knots when prep was rushed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Grays Fast Without Pigment</h3>
          <p>
            Left unprotected, cedar's warm red-brown surface oxidizes to silver-gray within a single season of sun. The only thing that stops UV is <strong>pigment</strong>. A clear sealer won't do it. That's the central reason this guide steers you toward semi-transparent cedar-toned stains: enough trans-oxide or iron-oxide pigment to block UV and hold the color, while still letting cedar's grain show through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transparent vs Semi-Transparent vs Solid on Cedar</h3>
          <p>
            The single biggest decision on cedar is opacity. Too little pigment and the wood grays; too much and you bury the grain that made you choose cedar in the first place. Here's how the three levels actually behave on cedar.
          </p>

