        <div id="how-to-stain" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How to Stain a Cedar Deck: Step by Step
          </h2>
          <p>
            Cedar rewards patience and punishes shortcuts. The two failures we see most often on cedar are staining new wood before it's ready (over the mill glaze) and flooding a film finish onto soft, damp fibers. Follow these six steps and your cedar will take the stain evenly and hold its color.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 1: Let New Cedar Dry — and Deal With the Mill Glaze</h3>
          <p>
            Fresh cedar from the yard is usually too wet to absorb stain and carries a smooth <strong>mill glaze</strong> — the slightly burnished, faintly waxy surface left by the planer's blades. Staining over it is the number-one reason new cedar decks fail early: the stain beads up and never penetrates. Let new cedar acclimate for two to four weeks of dry weather until it falls below roughly 15% moisture content, then plan to break that glaze in Step 3. The classic field test: sprinkle water on a board. If it soaks in within a minute or two, the wood is ready; if it beads, it needs more drying time and glaze removal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 2: Clean and Strip Old Finish</h3>
          <p>
            Remove dirt, mildew, pollen, and any failing old stain with a dedicated wood cleaner or a diluted oxygen-bleach solution and a stiff brush. Avoid chlorine bleach — it damages cedar's lignin and leaves it looking bleached and fuzzy. Rinse with a garden hose or a pressure washer kept under 1,200 PSI; cedar is soft and high pressure will gouge and furr the grain instantly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 3: Brighten and Lightly Sand</h3>
          <p>
            After cleaning, apply a wood <strong>brightener</strong> (oxalic acid) to neutralize the cleaner, restore cedar's natural reddish pH, and open the pores for better absorption. Once dry, give the surface a <strong>light sanding</strong> with 60–80 grit — just enough to knock down the mill glaze on new boards and any raised grain. Don't over-sand; cedar is soft and you only need to scuff the surface, not remove material. Sweep and vacuum all dust afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 4: Wait for Full Dry, Then Test Absorption</h3>
          <p>
            Cedar dries slower than it looks, especially the shaded undersides of boards. Give the deck a minimum of 48 hours of dry weather after washing and brightening — longer if it's humid. Confirm with a moisture meter (target below 15%) or the water-bead test from Step 1. Staining damp cedar traps moisture and causes early peeling, blotching, and mildew.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 5: Apply a Thin, Even Coat</h3>
          <p>
            Work in the shade or on an overcast day at 50–85°F — direct sun makes stain flash off before it can soak into cedar. Apply with a sprayer, pad, or brush, working two or three boards at a time from end to end to keep a wet edge and avoid lap marks. For penetrating oils like Ready Seal and Cabot, let the wood take what it wants and wipe back any pooling after a few minutes — cedar is thirsty but excess oil won't cure on the surface. For semi-transparents like TWP and DEFY, apply two thin wet-on-wet coats rather than one heavy one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Step 6: Let It Cure and Reach True Color</h3>
          <p>
            Keep foot traffic and water off the deck for 24–48 hours. Remember that several products — Ready Seal especially — keep developing color for up to two weeks as the oil cures, so don't judge the final tone on day one. Once cured, cedar that's been properly stained will shed water in tight beads and glow with that warm reddish color you were after.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Surface Prep for Cedar: Where Most Jobs Are Won or Lost
          </h2>
          <p>
            On cedar, preparation isn't a chore you do before the real work — it <em>is</em> the work. A premium stain on poorly prepped cedar will underperform a budget stain on properly prepped cedar every time. The two cedar-specific traps are the mill glaze on new wood and high-pressure washing that destroys the soft grain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Mill Glaze Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Brand-new cedar looks ready to stain, but that smooth, slightly shiny surface is exactly what keeps stain from soaking in. You cannot solve mill glaze by buying a better stain — it has to be physically removed by brightening and light sanding (Step 3 above). If you take one thing from this guide for a new cedar deck, it's this: break the glaze first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Moisture Test</h3>
          <p>
            Cedar must be dry before staining — below about 15% moisture content. Even after several dry days, shaded boards and undersides hold water. Use a moisture meter (about $30) or tape a one-foot square of clear plastic to the deck for a few hours; condensation on the underside means the wood is still too wet to stain.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-6">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Cedar Mistakes That Ruin Your Stain Job</p>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-red-900 text-sm">
              <li>• Staining new cedar over the mill glaze (stain beads and never penetrates)</li>
              <li>• Using a clear sealer and expecting it to stop graying (only pigment does)</li>
              <li>• High-pressure washing soft cedar (gouges and furs the grain)</li>
              <li>• Using chlorine bleach instead of oxygen bleach (bleaches and damages fibers)</li>
              <li>• Staining damp cedar before it drops below 15% moisture</li>
              <li>• Flooding a film-forming acrylic onto soft cedar (peels and traps tannins)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="oil-vs-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Oil vs Water-Based on Cedar
          </h2>
          <p>
            This is the question every cedar owner asks, and the honest answer is "it depends on what you value" — but the trade-offs on cedar specifically are clearer than on most woods.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Oil-based penetrating stains</strong> are the traditional choice for cedar, and for good reason. Cedar's soft, porous fibers drink oil deep into the wood, where it conditions the grain and weathers by fading gradually rather than peeling. Oils live in harmony with cedar's natural extractives, so tannin bleed is rare, and products like TWP 1500 Cedartone, Cabot Australian Timber Oil, and Armstrong-Clark deliver the richest, warmest cedar tones we tested. The downsides are practical: longer dry and cure times, stronger odor, mineral-spirit cleanup, and some oils warm or amber the color as they age.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Water-based stains</strong> used to be a poor fit for cedar — they sit higher on the surface and cheap acrylics fade fast — but the best modern formulas have largely caught up. DEFY Extreme Cedar Tone uses zinc-oxide nanoparticles as a microscopic UV screen and held cedar's color better than any other water-based product in our test. Water-based stains bring real advantages: low VOCs, low odor, soap-and-water cleanup, faster recoat windows, and colors that don't amber over time. The catch on cedar is that they're <strong>less forgiving of mill glaze</strong> — prep has to be right or they won't penetrate. Choose oil for maximum penetration and richness; choose water-based for low odor, easy cleanup, and color that stays true.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            How to Keep Cedar From Going Gray
          </h2>
          <p>
            Graying is the default fate of unprotected cedar — it's UV breaking down the surface fibers and oxidizing them to silver. Some owners love the weathered-gray look and let it happen, but if you want that warm reddish color, here's how to defend it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use pigment, not just sealer.</strong> The only thing that blocks UV is pigment. A clear waterproofer adds water repellency but offers almost no UV defense, so cedar will gray right through it. A semi-transparent cedar-toned stain with trans-oxide or iron-oxide pigment is what actually holds the color — that's the whole reason this guide centers on semi-transparents.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Stay on a schedule.</strong> Color life depends on the product and exposure. High-pigment oils and DEFY's zinc system run two to three years between coats; budget oils like Thompson's want an annual refresh. Horizontal deck floors fade faster than vertical fences and siding, which see less sun and abrasion. Don't wait for full gray — a maintenance coat applied before failure is a quick wash-and-reapply.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Restore before you stain.</strong> If your cedar has already grayed, don't stain over it — a wood brightener or oxalic-acid cleaner dissolves the oxidized surface layer and brings back a remarkable amount of the original warm color before you apply stain. Cedar that's restored first takes stain more evenly and looks dramatically better than cedar stained over gray.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep it clean.</strong> Dirt, pollen, and especially mildew trap moisture against the wood and accelerate both graying and finish failure. A sweep and a gentle wash each spring extends the life of any stain on cedar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            How We Tested: Real Cedar, 12 Months, Real Weather
          </h2>
          <p>
            Our cedar stain testing used the wood the recommendations are for. We built matching test surfaces from genuine western red cedar — a south-facing cedar deck section, a run of cedar privacy fence, and a panel of cedar lap siding — so we could see how each product behaved on both horizontal and vertical cedar. Every surface received identical, careful prep: cleaning with an oxygen-bleach solution, an oxalic-acid brightener to restore cedar's pH and open the grain, light 60–80 grit sanding to break the mill glaze on the new boards, and drying to under 15% moisture content verified with a calibrated meter.
          </p>
          <p>
            Each stain was applied per its manufacturer's directions with the correct method — penetrating oils flooded and wiped back, semi-transparents applied as thin wet-on-wet coats — during overcast weather to avoid flash-off on the soft wood. We allowed full cure (up to two weeks for the slower oils) before exposure testing began.
          </p>
          <p>
            The surfaces then faced a full year of unprotected exposure: direct south-facing sun, rain, snow, and freeze-thaw cycling, plus simulated foot traffic on the deck section from a weighted impact tester. Monthly evaluations tracked color retention of cedar's warm tone with a digital colorimeter, water repellency with a standardized bead test, mildew and graying via visual inspection of the shaded north-facing boards, and any peeling, blotching, or tannin bleed under a 10x loupe — paying special attention to the areas around knots where cedar discoloration starts.
          </p>
          <p>
            At twelve months we documented each surface and ranked the products on how well they preserved cedar's natural reddish color, penetrated the soft grain, resisted graying and mildew, and recoated. The seven here are the only ones that earned a clear recommendation for cedar. Several others we tested either grayed prematurely, peeled on the soft wood, or showed tannin discoloration around knots — and didn't make the list.
          </p>
