        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best stain for a cedar deck?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">For most cedar decks, a penetrating semi-transparent stain in a cedar tone is the best choice — it preserves the wood's natural reddish color while delivering real UV protection. Ready Seal Natural Cedar is our top overall pick for its foolproof application and deep penetration into soft cedar. TWP 1500 Cedartone is the best traditional semi-transparent, and DEFY Extreme Cedar Tone is the best water-based option. Clear sealers look great briefly but gray quickly because they lack the pigment cedar needs for UV defense.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use a transparent or semi-transparent stain on cedar?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Semi-transparent is the sweet spot for cedar. Transparent and clear finishes show off the grain beautifully but carry too little pigment to block UV, so cedar grays within a season or two. Solid stains last longer but hide the very grain and warm color that make cedar worth having. Semi-transparent stains keep the grain and knots visible while adding enough pigment to slow graying and preserve cedar's reddish-brown tone for years.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is oil-based or water-based stain better for cedar?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Oil-based penetrating stains traditionally suit cedar best because they soak deep into the soft, porous fibers, condition the wood, and weather by fading rather than peeling — TWP, Cabot Australian Timber Oil, and Armstrong-Clark excel here. Modern water-based stains have closed much of the gap: DEFY Extreme uses zinc-oxide nano UV blockers to hold cedar's color while offering low odor and soap-and-water cleanup. Choose oil for maximum penetration and richness, water-based for low VOCs and easy cleanup.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I keep my cedar deck from turning gray?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Graying is UV damage to the surface fibers, and only pigment stops it — clear sealers won't. Pick a semi-transparent stain with trans-oxide or iron-oxide pigments in a cedar tone, keep the deck clean so mildew and dirt don't trap moisture, and reapply on schedule (every 1–3 years depending on the product and exposure). If your cedar has already grayed, a wood brightener or oxalic-acid cleaner restores much of the original color before you stain.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to wait before staining a new cedar deck?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. New cedar usually needs a few weeks to dry to under about 15% moisture content so the stain can penetrate, and it carries a smooth mill glaze from the planer that repels stain. Clean and brighten the wood, then lightly sand to break the mill glaze and open the grain before staining. Test absorption by sprinkling water on a board — if it beads instead of soaking in, the wood isn't ready and the stain won't penetrate either.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
